## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of advanced bit-twiddling for permutation of set bits. Evaluates ability to craft constant-time logic without loops and to reason about edge cases of the last permutation.

## Key Concepts

- Next higher permutation with fixed popcount
- Edge-case handling (max permutation -> 0)
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts; no loops or branches
- Efficient 64-bit handling and overflow behavior

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
  uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
  uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
  uint64_t ones = ripple & -ripple;
  uint64_t next = ripple | (((ones ^ ripple) >> 2) / smallest);
  return next;
}
```

## Examples

- `nextBitPermutation64(0b0011)` returns `0b0101` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b1100)` returns `0b10000` (next permutation with 2 set bits)
- `nextBitPermutation64(0b111000)` returns `0b1000011` (next permutation with 3 set bits)

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about cross-half bit patterns and leverage a reversal primitive to detect palindromicity without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit representations split across two 64-bit halves
- Cross-half bit equality, palindrome concept
- Using an existing reversal primitive to avoid per-bit work

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
int isPalindrome128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t hi_rev, lo_rev;
  reverse128(hi, lo, &hi_rev, &lo_rev);
  return (hi == hi_rev && lo == lo_rev) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

